  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1246 AM MDT Mon Aug 17 2026\n\nThe monsoon continues today and, like last night, the models did \nanother bad job with the night time convection. There is less than \nlast night though with the air mass being drier than last night. \nBut, what I wouldn't give for a nice, strong cold front to push \nthrough and vanquish the monsoon until next summer. But, we have to \nplay the hand we are dealt so here we go.\n\nMoisture will increase across the area today, although not be a lot. \nPrecipitable water values today look to generally run anywhere from \n100 to 140 percent of normal, not excessively high. The result will \nbe a somewhat greater coverage of showers and storms that yesterday. \nMost guidance is also fairly consistent in keeping the most numerous \nstorms across the northern half of the area, with mainly dry \nconditions persisting in southern Wyoming. Not saying there won't be \nany there, but the chance is only around 1 out of 10. The best \nchance looks to be across the northern border where the chance is \naround 1 out of 3. As for hazards, dew point depressions are over 40 \ndegrees so there could be some strong wind gusts. There could also \nbe some locally heavy rain but there is steering flow today and that \nshould help mitigate the flooding threat; it is not zero but it is \nlow. With somewhat higher thicknesses, temperatures should be a \ncouple of degrees warmer than yesterday, fairly close to what is \nnormal for mid August. Some convection lingers into as well, but \nplacement is uncertain given the poor model performance with the \nmonsoon.\n\nTuesday looks like the most active day for this round of the \nmonsoon. The highest precipitable water values are not the highest, \nagain topping out around 140 percent of normal. However, it is the \nmost widespread. As a result, just about all locations will have at \nleast a 1 in 5 chance of a shower or thunderstorm. Just about \neverything else looks similar to today, the main threat would be \nstrong wind gusts, with a lesser chance of minor flooding with \ndecent steering flow mitigating that threat somewhat. A few showers \nmay linger into Tuesday night as well, but probably fewer than on \nMonday night. Temperatures should also run fairly similar to Tuesday.\n\n\nDrier air will then overspread most of the area for Wednesday and \nThursday. Precipitable water values fall back to below normal levels \nfor most of the area on Wednesday and even into Thursday as the \nmoisture gets suppressed to the south. The chance of convection on \nthese days is very small, less than 1 out 10 for all locations. \nThere is a slightly better chance of a shower or storm on Thursday, \nbut it would likely be restricted to the mountains. With the \nassociated ridge building northward, temperatures will be on the \nrise as well. The forecasted temperatures may be a few degrees too \ncool as well. Most of the lower elevations East of the Divide will \nprobably see high temperatures into the 90s possibly as early as \nWednesday and almost definitely from Thursday through at least \nSaturday. Some of the warmer spots could be in the upper 90s Friday \nor Saturday.\n\nThe chance of showers and storms also begins to rise on Friday as \nmonsoonal moisture moves back into the area. Most activity would be \nconfined to in and near the mountains on Friday though. Saturday may \nbe a day that needs to be watched. Some guidance shows precipitable \nwater values climbing to over 150 percent of normal, possibly \napproaching 200 percent of normal. There is steering flow, so this \nshould mitigate the flooding threat somewhat. I would not be \nsurprised to some sort of Excessive Rainfall Outlook for this day \nthough. Above normal moisture also looks to persist into Sunday and \nearly next week, keeping the chance of showers and thunderstorms in \nthe forecast.",
